  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` for the dusk desert canyon. `typecheck` passes, but I could not run or look at the scene (no shell or screenshots here). Colours, lighting, fog and the camera path are therefore untested in a browser.\n\n- **Canyon:** the terrain is a flat-shaded procedural mesh, built in chunks. Terraced sandstone walls rise on both sides of a winding dry riverbed. They have banded strata colours, dust on the ledges and a sandy floor. The shape repeats every 240 units along z, which lets the camera loop without a jump.\n- **Floor details:** instanced boulders and dead shrubs (clusters of thin sticks) are placed along that same repeating pattern.\n- **Sky and light:** a blue-violet to orange gradient sky, with a magenta band and a low sun near the horizon. A warm directional sun casts shadows, plus a hemisphere light and a weak neutral environment light for fill. Exponential fog uses a pinkish colour close to the horizon so distant walls fade toward the sky, and a mild bloom is added.\n- **Camera:** it follows a small hidden node that moves each frame, so it runs without user input. The node travels down the canyon at eye height, looks 28 units ahead, and drifts left and right on a sine wave. The loop is 40 seconds long.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nI chose the follow-camera approach because the docs say not to use `app.camera`.\n\nIf the first frames look wrong, check three things: whether the camera follows the hidden node as intended, whether the vertex colours come out as expected, and whether the floor is too dark. The low sun means the canyon walls may leave the floor in shadow.",
